\begin{abstract}
We study, for the two rational elliptic curves with complex multiplication of class number one and $j$-invariant $0$ and $1728$, whether residue-class data about a prime $p$ can predict divisibility properties of the group order $\#E(\FF_p)$ --- the quantity on whose smoothness the elliptic curve method of factorisation gambles. We prove a complete negative answer for $\Ezero : y^2 = x^3+1$ with complex multiplication by the Eisenstein integers, together with three exact structural laws.

First, a \emph{rational-torsion degeneracy}: $6 \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p)$ for every prime $p > 3$, proved without invoking the group law by exhibiting translation by the rational $3$-torsion point $(0,1)$ as an explicit fixed-point-free self-map of order three of the point set. Combined with the exact fact that a constant Boolean observable has empirical mutual information $0$ against every classifying statistic, this shows that the divisibility channels at levels $\ell \in \{1,2,3,6\}$ carry exactly zero bits on every finite sample of good primes. The null is a degeneracy of the \emph{event}, not of the statistic: the same functional attains $\log 2$, and the level-$5$ channel on the same curve attains $\log 2$ on an explicit two-prime sample. We classify the silent set exactly: $\ell \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p)$ holds unconditionally on the good primes if and only if $\ell \mid 6$, giving an all-or-nothing dichotomy between $0$ bits and a full bit.

Second, an \emph{exact inert collapse and residue dial}: for $p \equiv 2 \pmod 3$, $\#\Ezero(\FF_p) = p+1$ exactly, hence $a_p = 0$, and therefore $\ell \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p) \iff p \equiv -1 \pmod \ell$ for all $\ell \ge 1$. The same collapse holds for $\Egauss : y^2 = x^3+x$ on $p \equiv 3 \pmod 4$, so the mechanism is independent of the CM field; on the inert half, elliptic-curve factorisation on these curves reduces literally to the $p+1$ method. The trace vanishes exactly on the inert class for both curves, so in any finite sample the count of primes with $a_p = 0$ equals the count of inert primes identically. On the split half, visibility fails already at modulus $9$: $13$ and $31$ are both $\equiv 4 \pmod 9$ and split, yet $9 \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_{31}) = 36$ while $9 \nmid \#\Ezero(\FF_{13}) = 12$.

Third, a \emph{union-dilution law}: mixing a disjoint class-blind event of probability $b$ into a conditional channel leaves the weighted conditional variance unchanged while increasing the normaliser $\mu(1-\mu)$ below base rate $1/2$, so the squared correlation ratio satisfies $\eta^2(a+b) \le \eta^2(a)$ with exact factor $\mu_A(1-\mu_A)/\mu_U(1-\mu_U)$; the family is monotone in $b$ and the achievable factor set is exactly $(0,1]$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}

Factorisation algorithms of the ``one-prime-at-a-time'' family construct, for a hidden prime divisor $p$ of a composite $N$, an algebraic group $G_p$ and gamble on the smoothness of $\#G_p$. Pollard's $p-1$ method uses $G_p = \FF_p^\times$ with $\#G_p = p-1$; Williams' $p+1$ method uses a norm-one torus with $\#G_p = p+1$; the elliptic curve method uses $G_p = E(\FF_p)$, so that by Hasse's theorem
\[
  \#E(\FF_p) = p + 1 - a_p, \qquad |a_p| \le 2\sqrt{p}.
\]
The decisive advantage of the elliptic method is that $a_p$ varies with the curve. This raises the \emph{order-shadow question}: is there a computable statistic of $p$ --- in practice a residue class, computable from $N$ itself for small moduli --- carrying information about divisibility of $\#E(\FF_p)$ by a fixed small $\ell$? An affirmative answer would let one bias curve selection, and would be a structural weakness of factoring-based cryptography.

The most promising candidates are curves with complex multiplication, whose Frobenius traces are governed by abelian reciprocity and are therefore in principle residue-visible.

\subsection{The two CM curves}

Over $\QQ$ the two canonical CM curves are
\[
  \Ezero : y^2 = x^3+1 \quad (j=0,\ \End = \ZZ[\omega],\ \text{CM field } \QQ(\sqrt{-3}),\ \text{bad primes } 2,3),
\]
\[
  \Egauss : y^2 = x^3+x \quad (j=1728,\ \End = \ZZ[i],\ \text{CM field } \QQ(i),\ \text{bad prime } 2).
\]
We answer the order-shadow question for $\Ezero$ in the negative, with $\Egauss$ as the field-independence control, and isolate three mechanisms: \emph{degeneracy} (the uniformly visible divisibility is unconditional, hence carries zero bits), \emph{collapse} (where a residue class does dictate divisibility, the order is exactly $p+1$ and the method is the $p+1$ method), and \emph{invisibility} (where the arithmetic genuinely varies, the trace is not a function of $p$ modulo $9$). A fourth, purely statistical law --- union dilution --- explains why measurements of union events systematically understate conditional effects.

\subsection{Notation}

For $A,B \in \FF_p$ with $-16(4A^3+27B^2) \ne 0$ set
\[
  \Aff(A,B) = \{(x,y) \in \FF_p^2 : y^2 = x^3+Ax+B\}, \qquad \#E_{A,B}(\FF_p) = \#\Aff(A,B)+1,
\]
and $a_p = p+1-\#E_{A,B}(\FF_p)$. Write $\#\Ezero(\FF_p) := \#E_{0,1}(\FF_p)$ and $\#\Egauss(\FF_p) := \#E_{1,0}(\FF_p)$. A prime $p>3$ is \emph{good} for $\Ezero$; it is \emph{inert} when $p \equiv 2 \pmod 3$ and \emph{split} when $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$. For $\Egauss$, inert means $p \equiv 3 \pmod 4$.

\section{A counting principle for free cyclic self-maps}

\begin{lemma}[Free-iterate counting]\label{lem:free}
Let $\alpha$ be a finite set, $n \ge 1$, and $f : \alpha \to \alpha$ a self-map with $f^{[n]} = \mathrm{id}$ and $f^{[k]}(x) \ne x$ for all $x \in \alpha$ and all $0 < k < n$. Then $n \mid \#\alpha$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $f^{[n]} = \mathrm{id}$, $f$ is invertible with inverse $f^{[n-1]}$ and defines a permutation $g \in \Sym(\alpha)$ with $g^k$ acting as $f^{[k]}$. Then $g^n = 1$ and $g$ has order exactly $n$, since a smaller order $d$ would give a fixed point of $f^{[d]}$. The subgroup $\langle g \rangle$ has order $n$, and freeness of the iterates says precisely that all point stabilisers are trivial. A free action of a group of order $n$ splits $\alpha$ into orbits of size $n$, whence $n \mid \#\alpha$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
For $n=3$ freeness of $f$ alone suffices: if $f^{[2]}(x)=x$ then $f(x)=f^{[3]}(x)=x$.
\end{remark}

\section{Rational-torsion degeneracy on the $j=0$ curve}

The curve $\Ezero$ carries the rational point $T=(0,1)$ of order $3$ and the rational point $(-1,0)$ of order $2$; both persist under reduction at good primes. We convert the $3$-torsion into a counting statement using explicit rational maps, so that no group law is required.

\begin{definition}\label{def:tau}
Over a field $F$ of characteristic $\ne 2,3$ set, for $x \ne 0$,
\[
  \tau(x,y) \;=\; \Bigl(\ \frac{2(1-y)}{x^{2}},\ \ \frac{y-3}{y+1}\ \Bigr).
\]
\end{definition}

The second coordinate is a M\"obius transformation of order three; the first comes from the chord through $(x,y)$ and $T$, of slope $(y-1)/x$, after applying the curve relation.

\begin{lemma}[Well-definedness]\label{lem:wd}
If $y^2 = x^3+1$ and $x \ne 0$, then $y-1 \ne 0$ and $y+1 \ne 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $y = \pm 1$ then $x^3 = y^2-1 = 0$, so $x = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Stability]\label{lem:stab}
If $y^2 = x^3+1$ and $x \ne 0$, then $\tau(x,y)$ again lies on the curve and has non-zero first coordinate.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $X = 2(1-y)/x^2$ and $Y = (y-3)/(y+1)$, clearing denominators reduces $Y^2 - X^3 - 1$ to a polynomial multiple of $y^2-x^3-1$, namely with cofactor $-8(1-y)(x^3-1+y^2)$ after clearing. Non-vanishing of $X$ follows from Lemma~\ref{lem:wd} and $\operatorname{char} F \ne 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Order three]\label{lem:order3}
Under the same hypotheses,
\[
  \tau^{[2]}(x,y) = \Bigl(\frac{2x}{y-1},\ \frac{-(y+3)}{y-1}\Bigr), \qquad \tau^{[3]}(x,y) = (x,y).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The $y$-coordinate iterates as the M\"obius map $y \mapsto (y-3)/(y+1)$, whose cube is the identity; the $x$-coordinate follows from Lemma~\ref{lem:stab} with the curve relation used once at each step.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Freeness]\label{lem:free3}
If $\operatorname{char} F \nmid 6$, then $\tau(x,y) \ne (x,y)$ for every curve point with $x \ne 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Equality of first coordinates gives $2(1-y) = x^3 = y^2-1$, i.e.\ $(y+3)(y-1)=0$, so $y=-3$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:wd}. Equality of second coordinates gives $y^2 = -3$. Together $9 = -3$, i.e.\ $12=0$, impossible.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rational $3$-torsion degeneracy]\label{thm:three}
For every prime $p>3$, $\ 3 \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Define $\sigma$ on $\Ezero(\FF_p) = \Aff(0,1) \cup \{\infty\}$ by
\[
  \infty \mapsto (0,1) \mapsto (0,-1) \mapsto \infty, \qquad (x,y) \mapsto \tau(x,y)\ \ (x \ne 0),
\]
noting that on the fibre $x=0$ the only points are $(0,\pm 1)$, since $y^2 = 1$ factors as $(y-1)(y+1)=0$. By Lemmas~\ref{lem:stab}--\ref{lem:order3}, $\sigma$ is well defined with $\sigma^{[3]} = \mathrm{id}$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:free3} and $p>3$ it is fixed-point free. Lemma~\ref{lem:free} with $n=3$ concludes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Full $6$-torsion degeneracy]\label{thm:six}
For every prime $p>3$ we have $2 \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p)$, hence $6 \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p)$, and consequently $a_p \equiv p+1 \pmod 6$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $y^2=f(x)$ with $f$ a separable cubic over $\FF_p$, $p$ odd, the order is even if and only if $f$ has a root in $\FF_p$: a root gives a point of order $2$, and conversely the involution $(x,y)\mapsto(x,-y)$ makes the affine count congruent modulo $2$ to the number of roots. Here $f(x)=x^3+1$ has the root $x=-1$ unconditionally, and $\disc(x^3+1) = -27 \ne 0$ for $p \ne 3$. Combine with Theorem~\ref{thm:three} and $\gcd(2,3)=1$.
\end{proof}

Numerically $\#\Ezero(\FF_p) = 6,12,12,12,18,12,24,30,36$ for $p = 5,7,11,13,17,19,23,29,31$.

\section{The inert collapse, field-independently}

\begin{theorem}[Inert collapse, $\QQ(\sqrt{-3})$]\label{thm:collapse3}
If $p \equiv 2 \pmod 3$ then $\#\Ezero(\FF_p) = p+1$ exactly, hence $a_p = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $e = (2p-1)/3 \in \ZZ$, so $3e = 2(p-1)+1$. For every $u \in \FF_p$, $u^{3e} = u^{2(p-1)} u = u$ by Fermat (trivially for $u=0$). Hence $u \mapsto u^3$ is a bijection of $\FF_p$ with inverse $u \mapsto u^e$. So for each $y$ the equation $x^3 = y^2-1$ has exactly one solution, $x = (y^2-1)^e$: the projection $\Aff(0,1)\to\FF_p$, $(x,y)\mapsto y$, is a bijection and $\#\Aff(0,1) = p$. Add the point at infinity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Inert collapse, $\QQ(i)$]\label{thm:collapse4}
If $p \equiv 3 \pmod 4$ then $\#\Egauss(\FF_p) = p+1$ exactly, hence $a_p = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $s(c) = \#\{y : y^2=c\} = \chi(c)+1$ with $\chi$ the quadratic character, $\chi(0)=0$. For $p \equiv 3 \pmod 4$ one has $\chi(-1) = -1$, so $s(c)+s(-c) = 2$ for all $c$. The cubic $g(x)=x^3+x$ is odd, so re-indexing $S = \sum_x s(g(x))$ by $x \mapsto -x$ gives $S = \sum_x s(-g(x))$, whence $2S = \sum_x\bigl(s(g(x))+s(-g(x))\bigr) = 2p$ and $S = p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Field independence]\label{thm:fieldindep}
Let $q \equiv 2 \pmod 3$ and $r \equiv 3 \pmod 4$ be primes of good reduction. Then $\#\Ezero(\FF_q) = q+1$, $\#\Egauss(\FF_r) = r+1$, and for every $\ell \ge 1$,
\[
  \ell \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_q) \iff \ell \mid q+1, \qquad \ell \mid \#\Egauss(\FF_r) \iff \ell \mid r+1 .
\]
\end{theorem}

Thus, on the inert half of either CM field, every smoothness question about the elliptic order is literally the corresponding question about $p+1$: the CM curve contributes nothing beyond the $p+1$ method, and the mechanism does not depend on the CM field.

\begin{theorem}[Inert dial]\label{thm:dial}
Let $p \equiv 2 \pmod 3$ and $\ell \ge 1$. Then $\ell \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p) \iff p \equiv \ell-1 \pmod \ell$. In particular $3^m \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p) \iff p \equiv 3^m-1 \pmod{3^m}$; e.g.\ $9 \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p) \iff p \equiv 8 \pmod 9$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse3} the order is $p+1$, and $\ell \mid n+1$ iff $n \bmod \ell = \ell-1$, since $(n+1)\bmod \ell = (n\bmod\ell+1)\bmod\ell$ and $n \bmod \ell + 1 \le \ell$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Residue visibility on the inert half]
If $p,q \equiv 2 \pmod 3$ and $p \equiv q \pmod \ell$, then $\ell \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p) \iff \ell \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_q)$.
\end{corollary}

That the sharpest instances occur at powers of $3$, the ramified prime of $\QQ(\sqrt{-3})$, is structural: ramification shrinks the $3$-adic part of the relevant conductor, pinning the Frobenius datum modulo $3^k$ by a small modulus. Outside the inert half this pinning disappears.

\begin{theorem}[No split-half dial at modulus $9$]\label{thm:nosplit}
There are primes $q,r$ with $q \equiv r \pmod 9$, both $\equiv 1 \pmod 3$, such that $9 \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_q)$ and $9 \nmid \#\Ezero(\FF_r)$; their traces are incongruent modulo $9$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $q = 31$, $r = 13$: both are $\equiv 4 \pmod 9$ and $\equiv 1 \pmod 3$. Counting gives $\#\Ezero(\FF_{31}) = 36$ and $\#\Ezero(\FF_{13}) = 12$, so $9 \mid 36$ and $9 \nmid 12$. The traces are $a_{31} = 32-36 = -4$ and $a_{13} = 14-12 = 2$, and $-4 \not\equiv 2 \pmod 9$.
\end{proof}

\section{The exact trace dichotomy}

\begin{theorem}[Trace dichotomy, $\QQ(\sqrt{-3})$]\label{thm:tr3}
For a prime $p>3$: $a_p(\Ezero) = 0 \iff p \equiv 2 \pmod 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) is Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse3}. ($\Rightarrow$): by Theorem~\ref{thm:six}, $a_p \equiv p+1 \pmod 3$, so $a_p = 0$ forces $3 \mid p+1$; as $p>3$ is prime, $p \not\equiv 0$, hence $p \equiv 2 \pmod 3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Trace dichotomy, $\QQ(i)$]\label{thm:tr4}
For an odd prime $p$: $a_p(\Egauss) = 0 \iff p \equiv 3 \pmod 4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) is Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse4}. ($\Rightarrow$): if $a_p = 0$ and $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$, then $-1 = i^2$ is a square and $x^3+x = x(x-i)(x+i)$ has three distinct roots, so the curve has full rational $2$-torsion and $4 \mid \#\Egauss(\FF_p)$. But $a_p=0$ gives $\#\Egauss(\FF_p) = p+1 \equiv 2 \pmod 4$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Atomic trace law]\label{cor:atomic}
For any finite sample $S$ of good primes,
\[
  \#\{p \in S: a_p(\Ezero)=0\} = \#\{p \in S: p \equiv 2 \!\!\pmod 3\}, \quad
  \#\{p \in S: a_p(\Egauss)=0\} = \#\{p \in S: p \equiv 3 \!\!\pmod 4\}.
\]
\end{corollary}

An empirical observation that the trace vanishes on about half a sample is thus not a statistical near-coincidence: it is an identity between two counts, exact at every finite sample size. Supersingularity of these two CM curves is a pure residue condition.

\section{The information-theoretic half}

\subsection{The empirical channel}

Let $\Omega$ be a finite non-empty sample, $c : \Omega \to \kappa$ a class statistic with finite value set, and $E : \Omega \to \{0,1\}$ a Boolean event. Under the counting measure put
\[
  P(k,b) = \frac{\#\{\omega : c(\omega)=k, E(\omega)=b\}}{\#\Omega}, \quad
  P_c(k) = \frac{\#\{c=k\}}{\#\Omega}, \quad P_E(b) = \frac{\#\{E=b\}}{\#\Omega},
\]
\[
  I(c;E) = \sum_{k}\sum_{b \in \{0,1\}} P(k,b)\,\log\frac{P(k,b)}{P_c(k)P_E(b)},
\]
the plug-in mutual information in nats, with $0\log 0 = 0$.

\begin{theorem}[Degeneracy law]\label{thm:zerobit}
If $E$ is constant on $\Omega$ then $I(c;E) = 0$ exactly, for every class statistic $c$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Say $E \equiv v$. Then $P_E(v)=1$, $P(k,v)=P_c(k)$, and $P(k,b)=0$ for $b \ne v$. Summands with $b \ne v$ vanish by the prefactor; those with $b=v$ equal $P_c(k)\log 1 = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Silence of the small levels]\label{thm:silence}
For every finite non-empty sample of primes $>3$, every class statistic, and every $\ell \in \{1,2,3,6\}$, the channel $[\ell \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p)]$ has $I = 0$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:six} makes the event identically true; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:zerobit}.
\end{proof}

Because a null is only as trustworthy as the statistic producing it, we record two non-vacuity facts.

\begin{proposition}[The functional attains a full bit]\label{prop:onebit}
On the two-point sample $\Omega=\{0,1\}$ with $c=\mathrm{id}$ and $E=\mathrm{id}$, $I(c;E)=\log 2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$P(1,1)=P(0,0)=1/2$, $P(1,0)=P(0,1)=0$, all marginals $1/2$; each non-zero cell contributes $\tfrac12\log 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The level-$5$ event is conditional]\label{prop:five}
$\#\Ezero(\FF_{29})=30$ and $\#\Ezero(\FF_5)=6$, so $5 \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_{29})$ while $5 \nmid \#\Ezero(\FF_5)$. On the sample $\{29,5\}$ labelled by itself, the level-$5$ channel has $I=\log 2$.
\end{proposition}

Hence the level-$3$ null is a property of the event --- a rational-torsion degeneracy --- and not of the measuring statistic.

\begin{theorem}[Silent-set classification]\label{thm:silentset}
For $\ell \ge 1$: $\ \ell \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p)$ for every prime $p>3$ if and only if $\ell \mid 6$. Hence the zero-bit locus of $\Ezero$ is exactly $\{1,2,3,6\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) is Theorem~\ref{thm:six}. ($\Rightarrow$) needs one prime: $\#\Ezero(\FF_5)=6$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[All-or-nothing dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
For every $\ell \ge 1$ exactly one of the following holds.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\ell \mid 6$, and the channel $[\ell \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p)]$ carries exactly $0$ nats on every finite sample of good primes and against every class statistic.
\item $\ell \nmid 6$, and for every good prime $q$ with $\ell \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_q)$ the two-prime sample $\{q,5\}$ makes the same channel carry exactly $\log 2$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Case 1 is Theorems~\ref{thm:silentset} and \ref{thm:zerobit}. In case 2, $\ell \nmid 6 = \#\Ezero(\FF_5)$, so $q$ and $5$ have opposite divisibility, the event coincides with the sample label, and Proposition~\ref{prop:onebit} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Torsion-silence principle, curve-uniform]\label{thm:torsionsilence}
Let $\Omega$ be a finite non-empty sample and suppose each $\omega \in \Omega$ carries a finite point set $X_\omega$ with a self-map $f_\omega$ satisfying $f_\omega^{[n]} = \mathrm{id}$ and having no point of period $k$ for $0<k<n$. Then $n \mid \#X_\omega$ for all $\omega$, and the channel $[\,n \mid \#X_\omega\,]$ has empirical mutual information exactly $0$ against every class statistic.
\end{theorem}

No hypothesis is placed on $n$, on the curve, or on its CM field: rational torsion produces silence, complex multiplication does not.

\section{The union-dilution law}

Fix a finite class set $\kappa$ with weights $w_k \ge 0$, $\sum_k w_k = 1$, and a profile $a_k = P(A \mid k)$. Write
\[
  \mu(a) = \sum_k w_k a_k, \quad V(a) = \sum_k w_k (a_k-\mu(a))^2, \quad
  \eta^2(a) = \frac{V(a)}{\mu(a)(1-\mu(a))},
\]
the squared correlation ratio of the binary channel. Let $B$ be class-independent and disjoint from $A$ with $P(B) = b$, so $P(A \cup B \mid k) = a_k+b$.

\begin{theorem}[Union dilution]\label{thm:dilution}
Assume $\mu(a)>0$, $b \ge 0$ and $\mu(a)+b \le 1/2$. Then $\eta^2(a+b) \le \eta^2(a)$, strictly if $b>0$ and $V(a)>0$, with exact factor
\[
  \frac{\eta^2(a+b)}{\eta^2(a)} = \frac{\mu_A(1-\mu_A)}{\mu_U(1-\mu_U)}, \qquad \mu_A = \mu(a),\ \mu_U = \mu(a)+b .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(i) Shift-invariance: $\mu(a+b) = \mu(a)+b$ since $\sum_k w_k = 1$, so $(a_k+b)-\mu(a+b) = a_k-\mu(a)$ and $V(a+b) = V(a)$. (ii) Monotonicity: $t \mapsto t(1-t)$ is strictly increasing on $[0,1/2]$, so $\mu_A(1-\mu_A) \le \mu_U(1-\mu_U)$, strictly if $b>0$. Divide the common numerator by the larger denominator.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotone family]\label{thm:monotone}
If $0 \le b_1 \le b_2$ and $\mu(a)+b_2 \le 1/2$, then $\eta^2(a+b_2) \le \eta^2(a+b_1)$, strictly if $b_1<b_2$ and $V(a)>0$.
\end{theorem}

Call $c$ an \emph{achievable dilution factor} if some honest two-class channel --- classes of weight $1/2$, probabilities $a_k \in (0,1)$, admixture $b \ge 0$ with $a_k + b < 1$, $V(a)>0$, $\mu(a)+b \le 1/2$ --- satisfies $\eta^2(a+b) = c\,\eta^2(a)$.

\begin{theorem}[{The achievable set is exactly $(0,1]$}]\label{thm:sharp}
Every achievable dilution factor lies in $(0,1]$, and every $c \in (0,1]$ is achieved.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The upper bound is Theorem~\ref{thm:dilution}; positivity holds since $V(a)>0$. For $c=1$ take $b=0$. For $0<c<1$ set $r = \sqrt{1-c} \in (0,1)$ and $\mu = (1-r)/2$, and take $a = (\mu+\mu/2,\ \mu-\mu/2)$ with weights $1/2$ and $b = 1/2-\mu>0$. Then $\mu(a)=\mu$, $V(a)=(\mu/2)^2$, $\mu_U = 1/2$, so the factor is $4\mu(1-\mu) = 1-r^2 = c$.
\end{proof}

Consequently ``union channel $\le$ conditional channel'' is universal and no constant below $1$ improves it. In the CM setting the dilution has an arithmetic source: the split half raises the unconditional base rate without adding class-conditional variation, so the union sits strictly below the undiluted inert-class reference; swapping $\QQ(\sqrt{-3})$ for $\QQ(i)$ changes nothing, as it must.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Exact point counting.} For modest primes, $\#E_{A,B}(\FF_p) = 1+\sum_{x}\bigl(1+\chi(x^3+Ax+B)\bigr)$ with $\chi(c)=c^{(p-1)/2}$; cost $O(p\log p)$. On the inert half the computation short-circuits to $p+1$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse3} or \ref{thm:collapse4}, an $O(1)$ answer.

\paragraph{Silent-set detection.} Compute $\gcd_p \#E(\FF_p)$ over a modest set of good primes; its divisors are the candidate silent levels. For $\Ezero$ the gcd stabilises at $6$ immediately, and Theorem~\ref{thm:silentset} says this is exact.

\paragraph{Channel information.} From the $|\kappa| \times 2$ contingency table, $I$ costs $O(\#\Omega + |\kappa|)$ after the point counts. The theory predicts a hard $0$ at every $\ell \mid 6$ and every modulus.

\paragraph{Dilution correction.} Given an observed union effect and the two base rates, the conditional effect is $\eta_A^2 = \eta_U^2\,\mu_U(1-\mu_U)/(\mu_A(1-\mu_A))$; apply before comparing against a threshold calibrated on a pure conditional channel.

\section{Discussion}

For the $j=0$ CM curve the order-shadow question has a clean negative answer, decomposing into three mechanisms. \emph{Degeneracy}: the uniformly residue-visible divisibility (levels $1,2,3,6$) is unconditional, hence exactly zero bits --- a fully visible, abelian, $p+1$-sourced congruence on the elliptic order can reveal nothing; the shadow is real only when the event is conditional. \emph{Collapse}: where a residue class does dictate divisibility, the order is exactly $p+1$ and the method is the $p+1$ method. \emph{Invisibility}: where the arithmetic genuinely varies, the trace is not a function of $p \bmod 9$.

The null is of a stronger type than ``we measured a small correlation'': the correlation is exactly zero by an identity, the cause is isolated (rational torsion, not complex multiplication), the cause is classified (silence iff $\ell \mid 6$, with an all-or-nothing dichotomy), and the statistic is proven non-degenerate on explicit samples.

A structural theme is that residue-visible phenomena sit at the \emph{ramified} prime of the CM field: for $\QQ(\sqrt{-3})$ the dial is sharpest at $9$ and $27$. Ramification shrinks the relevant conductor, so the Frobenius datum modulo a power of the ramified prime is pinned by a small modulus; at unramified primes it is not, and Theorem~\ref{thm:nosplit} is a concrete witness.

Limitations: the silent-set classification is proved for $\Ezero$; the curve-uniform Theorem~\ref{thm:torsionsilence} gives torsion $\Rightarrow$ silence in general but not the converse. Split-half invisibility is established at modulus $9$ by explicit counterexample. The dilution law is a finite-sample statistical theorem whose application requires that the auxiliary event be genuinely class-independent and disjoint.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Curve-uniform silent set.} Is $\{\ell : \ell \mid \#E(\FF_p) \text{ for all good } p\}$ the divisor set of $\#E(\QQ)_{\tors}$ for every $E/\QQ$? One inclusion is Theorem~\ref{thm:torsionsilence}; the converse needs, for each $\ell$ not dividing the torsion order, one good prime avoiding $\ell$ --- plausibly an effective Chebotarev statement about the mod-$\ell$ representation.
\item \textbf{Split-half dials.} Is there any modulus $m$ and level $\ell$ making $\ell \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p)$ a function of $p \bmod m$ on the split half? The parameterisation $4p = L^2+27M^2$ suggests not.
\item \textbf{Higher-arity dilution.} Sharp analogues for unions of several disjoint class-blind events, and for non-binary observables measured by an $r$-ary correlation ratio.
\item \textbf{Torsion silence beyond elliptic curves.} The argument applies verbatim to any family of finite sets with a free order-$n$ self-map: Jacobians, tori, class groups with a distinguished element of known order.
\item \textbf{Quantifying the non-silent levels.} For a random good prime, what is the mutual information between $p \bmod m$ and $[\ell \mid \#\Ezero(\FF_p)]$? Theorem~\ref{thm:dial} answers this exactly on the inert half; the split half should be governed by CM equidistribution, with the two halves combining as the dilution law predicts.
\item \textbf{A ramification principle.} Formulate and prove: for a CM elliptic curve with CM field $K$, the trace $a_p$ modulo $\lambda^k$ is a function of $p$ modulo a small modulus precisely when $\lambda$ ramifies in $K$.
\end{itemize}

